Hi X,

On Wednesday, November 5, 2014, Xulong Wang <emai...@gmail.com> wrote:
> A related question with your first suggestion. Local variables in the generated quantities block are actually all saved in the output, at least in my case.

None of your generated quantities are within a local block. Try putting something like this inside your generated quantities block, including the addition curly braces:
{
   real x;
   x <- 1;
}

x should be saved to output.

>
> However, seems there is an argument in the stan function, par, which will declare the parameters you want to save. Want to confirm with you the "par" argument in the stan or sampling function does not affect the sampling process, but only select parameters to be saved. Am I correct?

This is for RStan. We'll let some of the RStan users let you know what the behavior is.

>
> X.
>
> On Saturday, October 18, 2014 4:27:26 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > Three suggestions.
> >
> > 1.   You don't need to save yHat as a transformed parameter if you don't
> >      need it in the output.  You can make it a local variable in the model.
> >      Then you'd have to recompute it in the generated quantities block, but
> >      you can also do that as a local variable.  Then you don't output yHat.
> >
> > 2.   You don't want to try to set c[k] to cu[k] - cu[1].  That doesn't
> >      identify the cu because they can all shift and you get the same behavior.
> >      Instead, do something like declaring cu as pos_ordered[K-2], then setting
> >      c[1] to 0, and all c[k] to cu[k-1].
> >
> > 3.   You can use the non-centered parameterization for alpha.  Define
> >      alpha_raw ~ normal(0,1) and then define alpha <- muAlpha + sigma_alpha * alpha_raw.
> >      This is much faster when there's not much data to estimate alpha with.
> >      It may not matter here if alpha is well identified by the model.
> >
> > - Bob
> >
> >
> > On Oct 18, 2014, at 3:48 AM, Xulong Wang <emai...@gmail.com> wrote:
