I now managed to build a stan model using emscripten, the reason for me failing to build before was because I did not know what I was doing. To build a stan model using emscripten I performed the following steps:

Install emscripten (http://kripken.github.io/emscripten-site/docs/getting_started/downloads.html#sdk-download-and-install) and make sure it works using the test suit.
Install CmdStan (http://mc-stan.org/cmdstan.html) according to the instructions there (including building it).
Make a stan model file, here I used a standard Bernouli model (saved as bernouli.stan):
data {
  int<lower=0> n;
  int<lower=0,upper=1> y[n];
}
parameters {
  real<lower=0,upper=1> theta;
}
model {
  theta ~ beta(1,1);
  for (i in 1:n)
    y[i] ~ bernoulli(theta);
}
Compile it using cmdstan with standard g++, but before doing that make sure you have removed cmdstan/bin/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.o , cmdstan/bin/libstan.a and, in case you compiled the model before, the resulting Bernouli executable. (Here my cmdstan folder happened to be on my desktop and the bernouli.stan model was in the folder ../stan_test/)
~/Desktop/cmdstan> make ../stan_test/bernouli
resulting in  the following print out (has here removed some lines):
g++ -DBOOST_RESULT_OF_USE_TR1 -DBOOST_NO_DECLTYPE -DBOOST_DISABLE_ASSERTS -I src -I stan/src -isystem stan/lib/eigen_3.2.0 -isystem stan/lib/boost_1.54.0 -Wall -pipe -DEIGEN_NO_DEBUG   -c -O3 -o bin/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.o stan/src/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.cpp

ar -rs bin/libstan.a bin/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.o

g++ -DBOOST_RESULT_OF_USE_TR1 -DBOOST_NO_DECLTYPE -DBOOST_DISABLE_ASSERTS -I src -I stan/src -isystem stan/lib/eigen_3.2.0 -isystem stan/lib/boost_1.54.0 -Wall -pipe -DEIGEN_NO_DEBUG  -lpthread  -O3 -o ../stan_test/bernouli src/cmdstan/main.cpp -include ../stan_test/bernouli.cpp -Lbin -lstan
Copy this printout to a text editor and change the the following things
Replace g++ with emcc (if emcc is not on your path, you have to write out the whole path to it)
Replace ar with emar (again including the parth if necessary)
remove the dash (-) in front of the ar flag, that is -rs should become just rs .
remove the optimization by setting -O3 to -O0.
change -o ../stan_test/bernouli to -o ../stan_test/bernouli.js
This should result in something like the following:
~/Desktop/emsdk_portable/emscripten/master/emcc -DBOOST_RESULT_OF_USE_TR1 -DBOOST_NO_DECLTYPE -DBOOST_DISABLE_ASSERTS -I src -I stan/src -isystem stan/lib/eigen_3.2.0 -isystem stan/lib/boost_1.54.0 -Wall -pipe -DEIGEN_NO_DEBUG   -c -O0 -o bin/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.o stan/src/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.cpp

~/Desktop/emsdk_portable/emscripten/master/emar rs bin/libstan.a bin/stan/agrad/rev/var_stack.o

~/Desktop/emsdk_portable/emscripten/master/emcc -DBOOST_RESULT_OF_USE_TR1 -DBOOST_NO_DECLTYPE -DBOOST_DISABLE_ASSERTS -I src -I stan/src -isystem stan/lib/eigen_3.2.0 -isystem stan/lib/boost_1.54.0 -Wall -pipe -DEIGEN_NO_DEBUG  -lpthread  -O0 -o ../stan_test/bernouli.js src/cmdstan/main.cpp -include ../stan_test/bernouli.cpp -Lbin -lstan
Run this in the cmdstan base path and hopefully you will have produced a hefty 13 mb javascript file called bernouli.js.
If you want to creat something you can run, replace change -o ../stan_test/bernouli.js with -o ../stan_test/bernouli.html this will create a html file that includes and runs the javascript code. In this case we would just get a printout saying that Stan is missing any input, but anyway :) Here is a link to my compiled Stan program if you want to see how it looks:

There might be a much better way to do this, but this is what I managed. Some notes:
It should certainly be possible to change the make file included in CmdStan to do this automatically, I just thought it was easier to manually change the compile commands to see what was going on.
By using the modified commands above you create libstan.a and var_stack.o files that should probably be put somewhere else than in the CmdStan directory.
I managed to compile Stan using higher than -O1 optimization but when using -O2 I got an runtime error when running the javascript code (using bernouli.html):
ReferenceError: DOT$ZERO is not defined
I havn't actually tried if the resulting Stan model works when running the actual model...

