>
> option 1:
> real<lower=0> sigma;
>  sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
>
> option 2:
> real<lower=0> sigma;
> sigma ~ cauchy(0,5) T[0,];
>
>
> Regards,
> Sachintha.
>
>
> On Tuesday, October 23, 2012 4:04:24 AM UTC+11, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>
> On 10/22/12 3:20 AM, Joe King wrote:
>
> > What is the recommended way to make a "uninformative" prior for a variance ?
>
> It's a red herring as far as modeling goes.  Any choice
> of prior is making a modeling decision.  We tend to prefer
> weakly informative priors.
>
>  > My maths teacher told me about gamma (from
> > what you said it seems she is wrong, so I will print the attached and ask her about it today), but my brother said I
> > should use a Jeffrey's prior.I also read about a half-cauchy distribution !
>
> Half Cauchy is the easiest in Stan, because you don't even
> need the half part because we only need to compute the probability
> up to a proportion.  For instance, you can use something like:
>
>     real<lower=0> sigma;
>
> ...
>
>     sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);
>
