Awesome --- I had no idea Boost's PRNGs let you do that.

I take it you saved the same way you read, just using:

  file_output_stream << rng:

What gets saved is bigger than a seed, though, right?
So in order to make this useful for Stan, we'd need a way
to read that state back into Stan.

- Bob

> On Nov 12, 2014, at 12:45 PM, Marco Inacio <marcoig...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>
> On 14-11-10 10:42 PM, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>> Being able to dump
>>
>>   * PRNG state
>>
>>   * adaptation state (mass matrix, time discretization [i.e., step size])
>>
>>   * parameter state
>>
>> No fundamental problem, but the PRNG state is a bit tricky given
>> the kind of PRNG we use
> When I tryed to work on resume sampling, the PRNG was the least of the problems, I was able to save it using just streams and << operators like in the example bellow:
>
> #include <iostream>
> #include <sstream>
> #include <fstream>
> #include <string>
> #include "boost/random.hpp"
> #include "boost/generator_iterator.hpp"
>
> int main(int /*argc*/, char** /*argv*/)
> {
>  boost::ecuyer1988 rng1;
>  boost::ecuyer1988 rng2;
>
>  rng1(); rng1(); rng1();
>
>  std::string state;
>  {
>  std::ifstream myfile;
>  myfile.open ("example.txt");
>  myfile >> rng2;
>  myfile.close();
>  }
>
>  std::cout << "state: " << state << std::endl;
>  std::cout << "rngs state comparison: " << (rng1 == rng2) << std::endl;
>  std::cout << "random number from rng 1: " << rng1() << std::endl;
>  std::cout << "random number from rng 2: " << rng2() << std::endl;
>
>  return 0;
> }
>

