MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> On Nov 18, 2014, at 7:54 AM, Chanhee Lee <face...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>
> Hi,
>
> I am working on hidden markov model with rstan.
>
> As indicated by section 18.2 of stan reference 2.5, label switching hinders the correct estimation of the model frequently.

Label switching shouldn't affect estimation, just the
convergence monitoring.

The real problem is multimodality if you don't have
the states associated with your observations.

> In my current model, developed from example code of rstan, the outcome probability density is incorrectly estimated at almost 50% probability and have problem in convergence as well.

See above.  Does it work if you run just one chain for a long time
(up to label ID, that is)?

> Because I am currently testing with only two hidden state and two outcome probability, the failure would become more significant problem in future.

>
> I've tried to add some code to make an order of state by some parameters (here, prior_alpha) but that solution seems not working well. (the code is attached below.)

I don't think this is a good solution in general, because
you probably don't want to have such an ordering as part of
the model.

> Could anybody suggest a way to add appropriate constraints on the parameters? or to solve label switching problem?

There's not a good general solution here.  If the states
are highly separated you can reidentify with the posterior
by matching the estimates, but that won't help with multimodality.

- Bob

> data {
>   int<lower=1> K;  // num categories
>   int<lower=1> V;  // num words
>   int<lower=1> I;  // num inds
>   int<lower=1> T_unsup;  // num unsupervised items
>   int<lower=1,upper=V> u[I,T_unsup]; // unsup words
>   vector<lower=0>[K] alpha;  // transit prior
>   vector<lower=0>[V] beta;  // emit prior
>   int<lower=0> age[I,T_unsup]; // covariates for prior probability
> }
>
>
> parameters {
>   simplex[K] theta[K];  // transit probs
>   simplex[V] phi[K];    // emit probs
>   vector[K] prior_alpha; // constant for prior prob.
>   vector[K] prior_beta; // coefficient for age
> }
>
>
> model {
>   for(k in 1:K) {
>     prior_alpha ~ normal(0.3*k,0.1);
>   }
>   prior_beta ~ normal(0,0.1);
>   for (k in 1:K)
>     theta[k] ~ dirichlet(alpha);
>   for (k in 1:K)
>     phi[k] ~ dirichlet(beta);
>
>
>   for (i in 1:I){
>     // forward algorithm computes log p(u|...)
>     real acc[K];
>     real gamma[T_unsup,K];
>     real flag;
>
>
>     vector[K] prior;
>     vector[K] priorreg;
>
>
>     priorreg <- prior_alpha + prior_beta * age[i,1];
>     prior <- softmax(priorreg);
>
>
>     for (k in 1:K)
>       gamma[1,k] <- log(prior[k]) + log(phi[k,u[i,1]]);
>     for (t in 2:T_unsup) {
>       for (k in 1:K) {
>         for (j in 1:K)
>           acc[j] <- gamma[t-1,j] + log(theta[j,k]) + log(phi[k,u[i,t]]);
>         gamma[t,k] <- log_sum_exp(acc);
>       }
>     }
>     flag <- 0;
>     for (k in 2:K) {
>       if(prior_alpha[k] > prior_alpha[k-1]) { //
>         flag <- 1;
>       }
>     }
>     if (flag == 1) {
>       increment_log_prob(negative_infinity());
>     } else {
>       increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(gamma[T_unsup]));
>     }
>   }
> }

This isn't going to work the way you expect.  For efficient sampling
with HMC, Stan needs the density to have support at every parameter
configuration that satisfies the constraints declared in the parameters
block.

- Bob

