From u743bbacc59f4b25f at groups.google.com  Thu Nov 20 19:04:06 2014
From: u743bbacc59f4b25f at groups.google.com (Ben Goodrich)
Date: Thu, 20 Nov 2014 19:04:06 +0000
Subject: Re: is a "perfect" n_eff suspicious?
Message-ID: <5Dy6I_5-pbM.2oDfz1Pmge4J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <5Dy6I_5-pbM.Nh_bn3LsTrgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <5Dy6I_5-pbM.Nh_bn3LsTrgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/5Dy6I_5-pbM/m/2oDfz1Pmge4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A perfect n_eff is an overestimate, but hopefully not a bad overestimate. So, you are probably fine, although we can construct examples where it is not totally fine despite no divergent transitions. One such example is drawing from a multivariate t distribution with sufficiently large K (like 30) and sufficiently small degrees of freedom (like 5). The reason is that there is essentially no autocorrelation marginally but a lot of cross-parameter dependence due to the heavy tails.

Ben

P.S. Model:

data {
  int<lower=1> K;
  real<lower=0> nu;
}
transformed data {
  matrix[K,K] I;
  vector[K] zero;
  zero <- rep_vector(0.0, K);
  I <- rep_matrix(0.0, K, K);
  for(k in 1:K) I[k,k] <- 1.0;
}
parameters {
  vector[K] theta;
}
model {
  theta ~ multi_student_t(nu, zero, I);
}
generated quantities {
  real SS;
  SS <- dot_self(theta);
}

